namespace CSharpDojo.V3CB01_TaskMachinery;

public static class Split
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Wrong on purpose: because the method is async, the ArgumentException is
    /// captured onto the returned task instead of being thrown at the call site.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task<int> Bad(string? name, Task<int> work)
    {
        ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(name);
        return name.Length + await work;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Not async: validation throws at the call site, and the async part lives
    /// in a separate method that this one returns.
    /// </summary>
    public static Task<int> Good(string? name, Task<int> work)
    {
        ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(name);
        return GoodCoreAsync(name, work);
    }

    private static async Task<int> GoodCoreAsync(string name, Task<int> work) =>
        name.Length + await work;

    /// <summary>
    /// Nothing is awaited, so nothing needs a state machine: returning a
    /// completed task is cheaper and behaves identically.
    /// </summary>
    public static Task<int> NoStateMachine(int value) => Task.FromResult(value);
}

references.Challenge.cs:
// v3cb01_task_machinery / challenge - an async manual-reset event.

namespace CSharpDojo.V3CB01_TaskMachinery;

/// <summary>
/// The async analogue of ManualResetEventSlim. One field holds the current
/// generation of waiters: a completed task *is* the set state, which removes
/// any chance of a flag and a task disagreeing.
/// </summary>
public sealed class AsyncManualResetEvent
{
    private volatile TaskCompletionSource source;

    public AsyncManualResetEvent(bool initiallySet)
    {
        source = NewSource();
        if (initiallySet) source.TrySetResult();
    }

    public bool IsSet => source.Task.IsCompleted;

    /// <summary>
    /// Already set: the completed task comes straight back, no suspension.
    /// Otherwise the caller waits on the current generation, and a cancellable
    /// token cancels only this waiter.
    /// </summary>
    public Task WaitAsync(CancellationToken token = default)
    {
        Task current = source.Task;
        if (current.IsCompleted) return Task.CompletedTask;
        if (token.IsCancellationRequested) return Task.FromCanceled(token);
        if (!token.CanBeCanceled) return current;

        // WaitAsync registers and disposes the token registration for us, so a
        // long-lived token does not accumulate callbacks per waiter.
        return current.WaitAsync(token);
    }

    /// <summary>TrySetResult so a second Set() is a no-op rather than a throw.</summary>
    public void Set() => source.TrySetResult();

    /// <summary>
    /// Swap in a fresh generation, but only if the current one is finished -
    /// two concurrent Resets must not leave two generations visible, and a
    /// Reset racing a pending gate must not orphan its waiters.
    /// </summary>
    public void Reset()
    {
        while (true)
        {
            TaskCompletionSource current = source;
            if (!current.Task.IsCompleted) return;
            if (Interlocked.CompareExchange(ref source, NewSource(), current) == current) return;
        }
    }

    private static TaskCompletionSource NewSource() =>
        new(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
}

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

Work backwards from the log. `"suspended"` is written by the caller *after*
`RunAsync` returned, and `"resumed"` comes after it. So `RunAsync` must have
given control back before `"resumed"` — that is the definition of a suspension,
and it is caused by awaiting the gate.

Everything above `"suspended"` therefore belongs before the `await`, and
everything below it after.

## Hint 2

```csharp
public static async Task RunAsync(TaskCompletionSource<int> gate, List<string> log)
{
    log.Add("start");
    log.Add("sync-work");
    await gate.Task;
    log.Add("resumed");
    log.Add("done");
}
```

That is the whole method. The interesting part is being able to say why it
cannot be written any other way and still produce that order.

For `ContinuationRanInline`, compare `Environment.CurrentManagedThreadId`
captured inside the continuation with the one captured just before
`gate.SetResult`.

## Hint 3

`ContinuationRanInline` is the one with a decision in it. Whether the
continuation runs inline depends on how `gate` was created — and the gate is
handed to you, so your method must report what it observes rather than assume.

Write it so it works either way:

```csharp
int completer = 0, observed = -1;
Task recorded = gate.Task.ContinueWith(_ => observed = Environment.CurrentManagedThreadId,
    TaskContinuationOptions.ExecuteSynchronously);
completer = Environment.CurrentManagedThreadId;
gate.SetResult(1);
recorded.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
return observed == completer;
```

`TaskContinuationOptions.ExecuteSynchronously` is the `ContinueWith` analogue
of the inline behaviour — it asks to run on the completing thread, and is a
request rather than a guarantee (the runtime declines it if, for example, the
stack is too deep).

Left to you: whether blocking with `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` at the end is
acceptable here. In a test that is measuring thread identity, it is the honest
way to be sure the continuation finished; in production code the same line is
the thing this sector spent its time warning you about.

