exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

The entire testability of this comes from one parameter. `Task.Delay` has an
overload taking a `TimeProvider`; use it and the gate's fake clock controls
every wait.

Write `NextDelay` first as a pure function — it is the piece you will want to
unit test on its own, and having it separate keeps the loop readable.

## Hint 2

```csharp
for (int attempt = 0; ; attempt++)
{
    try
    {
        Attempts++;
        return await this.attempt(attempt).ConfigureAwait(false);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) { throw; }
    catch when (attempt + 1 < maxAttempts)
    {
        TimeSpan delay = NextDelay(attempt);
        Delays.Add(delay);
        await Task.Delay(delay, time, token).ConfigureAwait(false);
    }
}
```

Two catch clauses, in that order. The cancellation one must come first, or the
filtered one swallows an abandoned request and retries it.

## Hint 3

Three details that separate a real policy from a toy.

**Jitter.** Every client retrying at exactly 1s, 2s, 4s after a shared outage
produces synchronised thundering herds. Production policies randomise —
typically full jitter, `delay * random(0, 1)`. The gate wants a deterministic
schedule, so this one has none; know that it is missing.

**Which failures to retry.** Retrying a `400 Bad Request` is pointless, and
retrying a non-idempotent write is dangerous. A real policy takes a predicate.

**A ceiling.** `2^n` reaches absurd numbers quickly; production caps the delay
and often the total elapsed time as well, with the overall deadline as a linked
token.

Left to you: whether the final failure should be rethrown as-is or wrapped with
attempt context. Rethrowing preserves the type that callers catch on; wrapping
gives better logs. `throw;` plus a structured log line usually beats both.

