// v3cb05_async_streams / ex3 - demand-driven evaluation, measured.

namespace CSharpDojo.V3CB05_AsyncStreams;

public static class Lazy
{
    /// <summary>Records each value as it is produced, so the consumer's demand is visible.</summary>
    public static async IAsyncEnumerable<int> NumbersAsync(int count, List<int> produced)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        {
            await Task.Yield();
            produced.Add(i);
            yield return i;
        }
    }

    public static async Task<List<int>> TakeAsync(IAsyncEnumerable<int> source, int n)
    {
        var taken = new List<int>(n);
        if (n <= 0) return taken;

        await foreach (int item in source.ConfigureAwait(false))
        {
            taken.Add(item);
            if (taken.Count == n) break;      // stops asking; nothing more is produced
        }

        return taken;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Returning from inside the loop still runs the desugaring's finally, so
    /// the producer is disposed and its cleanup runs.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task<int> FirstMatchAsync(IAsyncEnumerable<int> source, Func<int, bool> match)
    {
        await foreach (int item in source.ConfigureAwait(false))
            if (match(item)) return item;

        return -1;
    }

    public static bool RanAhead(List<int> produced, int consumed) => produced.Count > consumed;
}

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

Two disposals, in a specific order. Your iterator holds a resource; the
consumer holds an enumerator. Breaking out of `await foreach` disposes the
enumerator, which resumes your iterator so *its* cleanup can run.

Write the iterator with `await using` and a `try`/`finally` and let the runtime
do the rest — then predict the order before running.

## Hint 2

```csharp
public static async IAsyncEnumerable<int> ReadAsync(List<string> log, int count)
{
    await using var resource = new Resource(log);
    log.Add("opened");
    try
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        {
            await Task.Yield();
            yield return i;
        }
    }
    finally { log.Add("finally"); }
}
```

`finally` runs before the `await using` disposes, because the `using` was
entered first — same last-in-first-out rule as synchronous code.

## Hint 3

The mechanism behind the ordering is worth spelling out, because it is not
obvious that a `finally` can run at all after a `break`.

When the consumer breaks, the desugaring's own `finally` calls
`enumerator.DisposeAsync()`. The generated enumerator sets a "disposing" flag
and resumes `MoveNext` at the suspension point. From the iterator's point of
view, the `yield return` behaves as if an exception were thrown there: control
unwinds through your `finally` blocks and `await using` declarations in reverse
order, and then the state machine completes the `DisposeAsync` task.

`Abandon` demonstrates the alternative. No `DisposeAsync`, no resumption, no
`finally`, no released resource — and unlike synchronous iterators, there is no
finaliser to save you. The file stays open until the process ends.

Left to you: whether to ever call `GetAsyncEnumerator` by hand. Sometimes you
must — merging two streams, peeking at the first item — and then `await using
var e = source.GetAsyncEnumerator(token);` is mandatory, not optional.

