## Hint 1

Two types, not one. `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` is a factory with a single method;
`IAsyncEnumerator<T>` is the cursor with `MoveNextAsync`, `Current` and
`DisposeAsync`. The compiler merges them into one class as an optimisation;
you do not have to.

Start with the enumerator — it holds all the state — and make the enumerable a
three-line wrapper.

## Hint 2

```csharp
public IAsyncEnumerator<int> GetAsyncEnumerator(CancellationToken token = default)
{
    CancellationTokenSource linked =
        CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(creationToken, token);
    return new Enumerator(count, load, linked);
}

// in the enumerator
public ValueTask DisposeAsync()
{
    linked.Dispose();
    return default;
}
```

That linking is exactly what `[EnumeratorCancellation]` generates for you, and
the disposal is exactly the leak from sector 3 if you forget it.

## Hint 3

The `MoveNextAsync` shape is sector 2's split, one more time:

```csharp
public ValueTask<bool> MoveNextAsync()
{
    linked.Token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    if (index + 1 >= count) return new ValueTask<bool>(false);
    return new ValueTask<bool>(AdvanceAsync());
}
```

If you can answer without awaiting — end of sequence, or a buffered value —
return a completed `ValueTask<bool>` and count it as a synchronous move.

Two contract details the gate checks. `MoveNextAsync` must return `false` after
the last item rather than throwing, because `await foreach` calls it exactly
once more than there are items. And `Current` must not be awaited or
recomputed: it is a property read of whatever the last successful move
produced.

Left to you: whether to throw from `Current` before the first move. The BCL is
inconsistent about this; returning `default` and documenting it is cheaper and
what the compiler-generated enumerators do.

